The neon lights whirred above the conference table, casting long shadows across the holographic displays showing live feeds from the warzone city outside. Elias sat at the table, gripping the edge of the leather-bound notebook until his knuckles turned white. There were lines he hadn’t written for years, cracks he expected would splinter, though he wasn’t entirely sure how deep they ran. 

On screen, a folder appeared—a matrix interface overlayed over standard corporate data. Where the public eye saw walls, Elias saw a map drawn in shadows. He knew the man who held the files wore a ring that summoned hidden data points whenever he blinked. The target was not random; it was the corporate shield protecting millions of dollars in payroll, patents, and supply roadmaps locked behind firewalls.

The red warning slowly blinked in traffic, waiting for his signature. No moment to waste. He didn’t need a Trojan before his hands landed; all he needed was the trust gap he’d never trale t yet. With a flick of his wrist, he triggered the remote access protocol. It was an automated wave of commands that flashed windows corporate employees used daily. he sat at the terminal, typing slowly, waiting for the keys to unlock. He knew it required authority he didn’t possess. He’d stolen the weapon of leverage. But hiding it wasn’t enough. The moment the system flooded, the locks hissed;
